title: 'max_runtime: 0 silently turns drain: true into a single run'
updated: 2026-08-13T02:22:06Z
---

Found in a bug hunt, reproduced.

Foundation.pm:621 -- `last if !$first && ( time - $loop_start ) >= $max_runtime;`

The same knob is both the per-command timeout and the whole drain's
wall-clock budget, so the documented "max_runtime: 0 disables the per-run
timeout entirely" makes this `>= 0`, true on every iteration.

Reproduction, identical board, drain: true, max_iterations: 3, agent moves
the board on every invocation:

    max_runtime: 60  ->  agent invocations this drain: 3
    max_runtime: 0   ->  agent invocations this drain: 1

Cost: an operator who follows the POD to let a long agent run to completion
also loses draining, with no message. The board advances one step per cron
tick instead of being drained, and nothing in the log or .karr.state says
why.

Fix direction: separate the two budgets, or special-case 0 as "no wall-clock
bound on the drain either".
